# Who reviews what. GitHub requests a review from the owners of every path a
# pull request touches; with "Require review from Code Owners" on in branch
# protection, their approval is what merges it.

*                                   @JordiParraCrespo

# The security-critical paths: who can sign in, what a role, a scope or an
# organization lets them do, how hard they can hit the API, what an error
# reveals, what the browser may run, and what CI's token can touch. A second
# look there means a second owner, and a later line replaces an earlier one
# rather than adding to it, so each line names both. Uncomment them once the
# project has that team (a team that does not exist makes the line an error).
#
# /packages/auth/                     @JordiParraCrespo @your-org/security
# /apps/api/src/auth/                 @JordiParraCrespo @your-org/security
# /packages/backend/authz/            @JordiParraCrespo @your-org/security
# /apps/api/src/authz/                @JordiParraCrespo @your-org/security
# /apps/api/src/roles/                @JordiParraCrespo @your-org/security
# /packages/shared/src/permissions/   @JordiParraCrespo @your-org/security
# /packages/shared/src/scopes/        @JordiParraCrespo @your-org/security
# flama:begin organizations
# /apps/api/src/organizations/        @JordiParraCrespo @your-org/security
# flama:end organizations
# /apps/api/src/throttling/           @JordiParraCrespo @your-org/security
# /packages/backend/core/src/filters/ @JordiParraCrespo @your-org/security
# /apps/api/src/config/               @JordiParraCrespo @your-org/security
# flama:begin web
# /apps/web/nginx.conf                @JordiParraCrespo @your-org/security
# flama:end web
# flama:plugins codeowners
# /.github/                           @JordiParraCrespo @your-org/security
